        <h2 id="quickstart">Quick start</h2>
        <p>Install the Windows agent from <a href="/downloads">Downloads</a>. It pairs itself with your workspace in the browser and puts <code>hashrelay</code> on your PATH. Then, in a new terminal:</p>
        <div class="code"><button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button><pre><code>hashrelay subscribe #builds
hashrelay publish #builds "Build complete" "212 tests passed in 48s"</code></pre></div>
        <p>The notification appears in Windows Notification Center on every device in the workspace subscribed to <code>#builds</code>. From CI or a server, create an API key in the dashboard and publish over HTTP:</p>
        <div class="code"><button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button><pre><code>curl -X POST @url/api/v1/notifications \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer hr_live_…" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"tags":["builds"],"title":"Build complete","body":"212 tests passed in 48s"}'</code></pre></div>

        <h2 id="concepts">Concepts</h2>
        <table class="table">
            <tr><th>Workspace</th><td>A tenant. Hashtags, keys, devices and notifications are isolated per workspace; nothing crosses the boundary.</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Hashtag</th><td>A subscription topic inside a workspace. Normalised to lowercase <span class="mono">[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}</span>; <code>#Builds</code> and <code>builds</code> are the same tag.</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Endpoint</th><td>A device (or headless listener) with a stable id. It holds a set of subscriptions and receives at most one notification per publication, however many of its tags match.</td></tr>
            <tr><th>API key</th><td>A tenant-bound publisher credential for trusted integrations. Shown once, stored hashed, revocable, with explicit <code>publish</code>/<code>subscribe</code> permissions.</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Device token</th><td>A credential issued to one endpoint through pairing. Installed clients never carry your publisher API key.</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h2 id="cli">CLI reference</h2>
        <p>The CLI stores its configuration per user in <span class="mono">%APPDATA%\HashRelay\config.json</span>. Exit codes: <code>0</code> success, <code>2</code> invalid input, <code>3</code> authentication, <code>4</code> server unavailable, <code>5</code> request rejected, <code>10</code> unexpected. Add <code>--json</code> to any command for machine-readable output.</p>
        <table class="table">
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay login</td><td>Pair this device with a workspace in the browser. Use <code>--key hr_live_…</code> for headless machines.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay configure --server URL</td><td>Point the CLI and agent at another HashRelay server.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay subscribe #tag…</td><td>Subscribe this device to one or more hashtags.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay unsubscribe #tag…</td><td>Remove subscriptions.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay subscriptions</td><td>List this device's subscriptions.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay publish #tag… "Title" ["Body"]</td><td>Publish. Also accepts <code>--tag</code>, <code>--title</code>, <code>--body</code>, <code>--url</code>, <code>--priority</code>, <code>--id</code>, and for rich content <code>--markdown</code>/<code>--markdown-file</code>, <code>--diagram</code>/<code>--diagram-file</code> (Mermaid) and <code>--image</code> (file or URL). Use <code>-</code> as a file to read stdin.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay listen [#tag…]</td><td>Stream notifications for this device to the console.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay test</td><td>Publish a test notification to this device and verify delivery.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay status</td><td>Show server, workspace, device and agent status.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay agent start|stop|status</td><td>Control the Windows tray agent.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">hashrelay logout</td><td>Forget the stored credential.</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h2 id="auth">Authentication</h2>
        <p>Send your credential as a bearer token. Tenant identity is resolved from the credential; the API never trusts a client-supplied tenant id.</p>
        <div class="code"><pre><code>Authorization: Bearer hr_live_…        # API key (dashboard → API keys)
Authorization: Bearer hr_dev_…         # device token (issued by pairing)</code></pre></div>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method get">GET</span><code>/api/v1/me</code></div>
        <p>Returns the tenant and credential the token resolves to.</p>

        <h2 id="publish">Publish API</h2>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method">POST</span><code>/api/v1/notifications</code> <span class="badge">publish</span></div>
        <div class="code"><pre><code>{
  "tags": ["builds", "ci"],          // 1–10 hashtags, '#' optional
  "title": "Build complete",         // 1–120 chars
  "body": "212 tests passed",        // optional, ≤ 1000 chars
  "url": "https://ci.example/run/42", // optional; opened when the toast is clicked
  "priority": "normal",              // low | normal | high
  "notificationId": "uuid",          // optional; makes retries idempotent
  "markdown": "## Details\n…",       // optional, ≤ 20 000 chars; rendered on the notification page
  "diagram": "graph LR\nA-->B",      // optional Mermaid source, ≤ 10 000 chars (else the first ```mermaid fence in markdown)
  "imageUrl": "https://…/i/….png"    // optional absolute image URL (see POST /api/v1/images)
}</code></pre></div>
        <p><strong>Rich content.</strong> A Windows toast shows plain text and one image, so <code>markdown</code> and <code>diagram</code> are rendered on a web page at <code>/n/{notificationId}</code> (members of the workspace only), which the toast opens when clicked. The toast body falls back to the first paragraph of the markdown. A diagram is also rendered to PNG on the server and shown inside the toast; the page renders it live with Mermaid, as it does any <code>```mermaid</code> fence in the markdown. Responses include <code>detailUrl</code> and <code>imageUrl</code>.</p>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method">POST</span><code>/api/v1/images</code> <span class="badge">publish</span></div>
        <p>Raw body upload with <code>Content-Type: image/png | image/jpeg | image/gif | image/webp</code>, at most 3 MB. Returns <code>201 {"id","url","bytes","contentType"}</code>; the URL is public but unguessable and is what you pass as <code>imageUrl</code>. From the CLI, <code>--image path.png</code> does this for you.</p>
        <p>Returns <code>202 Accepted</code>:</p>
        <div class="code"><pre><code>{
  "notificationId": "7a2c…", "acceptedAtUtc": "2026-10-31T12:00:00Z",
  "tags": ["builds","ci"], "title": "Build complete",
  "matchedEndpoints": 3, "deliveredLive": 2, "queued": 1, "duplicate": false
}</code></pre></div>
        <p>Re-sending the same <code>notificationId</code> with an identical payload returns <code>200</code> with <code>duplicate: true</code>; a different payload is rejected with <code>409</code>. The union of subscribers across all tags is matched and each endpoint receives exactly one notification. Endpoints that are offline keep a queued delivery that is replayed when they reconnect within @Model.ReplayWindowHours hours.</p>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method get">GET</span><code>/api/v1/notifications?limit=20</code></div>
        <p>Recent publications in the workspace with delivery counts.</p>

        <h2 id="endpoints">Endpoints &amp; subscriptions</h2>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method get">GET</span><code>/api/v1/endpoints</code></div>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method">POST</span><code>/api/v1/endpoints</code> <span class="badge">subscribe</span></div>
        <div class="code"><pre><code>{ "endpointId": "my-ci-runner", "platform": "linux", "name": "CI runner 1" }   → 201 (or 200 if it existed)</code></pre></div>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method get">GET</span><code>/api/v1/endpoints/{endpointId}/subscriptions</code></div>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method">PUT</span><code>/api/v1/endpoints/{endpointId}/subscriptions</code> <span class="badge">subscribe</span></div>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method">POST</span><code>/api/v1/endpoints/{endpointId}/subscriptions</code> <span class="badge">subscribe</span></div>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method del">DELETE</span><code>/api/v1/endpoints/{endpointId}/subscriptions?tags=a&amp;tags=b</code> <span class="badge">subscribe</span></div>
        <p>PUT replaces, POST adds, DELETE removes; each takes or returns <code>{"endpointId":"…","tags":["…"]}</code>. Single-tag forms <code>PUT/DELETE …/subscriptions/{tag}</code> return <code>204</code> idempotently.</p>

        <h2 id="pairing">Device pairing</h2>
        <p>Installed clients obtain a device token with an OAuth-style device flow, so they never hold a publisher key:</p>
        <div class="code"><pre><code>POST /api/v1/device/code   { "endpointId", "deviceName", "platform", "client": "agent" | "cli" }
  → { "deviceCode", "userCode": "KQ7M-3PXD", "verificationUrlComplete": "@url/pair?code=KQ7M-3PXD", "expiresInSeconds": 900, "intervalSeconds": 3 }

POST /api/v1/device/token  { "deviceCode" }
  → { "status": "pending" | "approved" | "denied" | "expired", "token": "hr_dev_…", "tenant": {…}, "credential": {…} }</code></pre></div>
        <p>The person approves the code at <a href="/pair">/pair</a>, choosing the workspace and whether the device may also publish. Approving again for the same endpoint replaces its previous token.</p>

        <h2 id="realtime">Realtime hub</h2>
        <p>Agents connect to the SignalR hub at <code>@HubMethods.Path</code> with their token (<code>Authorization</code> header or <code>?access_token=</code>). The server binds the endpoint identity in <code>Hello</code>, replays the backlog (queued deliveries, plus any handed to an earlier connection but never acknowledged) with <code>replayed: true</code>, and pushes:</p>
        <div class="code"><pre><code>// client → server
Hello({ endpointId, name, platform, agentVersion }) → { tenant, endpointId, subscriptions[], pendingReplay }
Subscribe(tags[]) / Unsubscribe(tags[]) → tags[]
Ack(deliveryId)

// server → client
NotificationReceived({ notificationId, tenantId, tags, title, body, publishedAtUtc, deliveryId, url, priority, tenantName, replayed,
                       imageUrl, detailUrl, rich, publisher: { id, name, type, channel } })
SubscriptionsChanged(tags[])
CredentialRevoked(reason)</code></pre></div>
        <p>Groups are per-endpoint routing addresses only. Subscriptions are persisted and are the sole authority for matching. A subscription takes effect for notifications published after it is created; earlier notifications are not replayed to it (read them with <code>GET /api/v1/notifications</code> or the endpoint's <code>/deliveries</code>).</p>
        <p><strong>Catch-up.</strong> Clients acknowledge every delivery they receive, replayed or not, and acknowledgement means "received", not "a toast was shown". The Windows agent shows live deliveries at once and folds a replayed backlog into one summary toast ("47 notifications while you were away"), showing at most three high-priority items on their own; the summary opens the Activity page.</p>
        <p><strong>Publisher.</strong> <code>publisher</code> is set by the server from the credential: a service account (its name and the type an admin declared), an API key (its label and declared type) or a paired device (its name, type <code>human</code>). <code>type</code> is one of <code>@string.Join(", ", PublisherTypes.All)</code> and is never inferred from MCP, REST or the message text; <code>channel</code> is the entry point (<code>@string.Join(", ", Channels.All)</code>). Notifications stored before attribution existed carry type <code>unknown</code>. The same object appears on <code>GET /api/v1/notifications</code> and the deliveries inbox.</p>

        <h2 id="errors">Errors &amp; limits</h2>
        <div class="code"><pre><code>{ "code": "validation_failed", "message": "The publication is not valid.", "traceId": "00-…", "details": { "title": ["Title must be 1–120 characters."] } }</code></pre></div>
        <table class="table">
            <tr><td class="mono">400 validation_failed</td><td>Malformed input; see <code>details</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">401 unauthenticated</td><td>Missing, unknown, revoked or expired credential.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">403 forbidden</td><td>Credential lacks the permission, or a device token acted outside its endpoint.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">404 not_found</td><td>Unknown endpoint in this workspace.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">409 conflict</td><td>notificationId reused with a different payload.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">429 throttled</td><td>Over @Model.RateLimit publications per minute per credential. Honour <code>Retry-After</code>.</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h2 id="mcp">MCP for AI agents</h2>
        <p>HashRelay is an <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" rel="noopener">MCP</a> server, so Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code and your own agents can publish notifications as a tool call. The endpoint speaks Streamable HTTP and is authenticated with a <strong>service account</strong> key.</p>
        <div class="endpoint"><span class="method">POST</span><code>@Model.McpUrl</code> <span class="badge">Bearer hr_svc_…</span></div>
        <p>Create the account under <a href="/dashboard/service-accounts">Dashboard → Service accounts</a>. Unlike an API key, a service account carries a policy that every request is checked against:</p>
        <table class="table">
            <tr><td class="mono">permissions</td><td><code>notifications:publish</code>, <code>notifications:read</code>, <code>endpoints:read</code>, <code>subscriptions:write</code>, <code>images:write</code>. New accounts get publish only.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">allowed hashtags</td><td>Glob patterns such as <code>agent-*</code> or <code>ci/*</code>. A publish to any other hashtag is refused with <code>403</code> and logged.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">link policy</td><td><code>url</code> and <code>imageUrl</code> must be https, optionally restricted to listed hosts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">quota</td><td>Publishes per hour across all of the account's keys; over it, <code>429 throttled</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">audit</td><td>Every publish, refusal, key issue and revocation is listed on the account's page. Disabling the account suspends all keys at once.</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p>The same key also works for the REST API above, with the same policy applied.</p>
        <div class="code-title">Claude Code</div>
        <div class="code"><button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button><pre><code>claude mcp add --transport http hashrelay @Model.McpUrl \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer hr_svc_…"</code></pre></div>
        <div class="code-title mt-2">Cursor, VS Code, Windsurf (mcp.json)</div>
        <div class="code"><button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button><pre><code>{
  "mcpServers": {
    "hashrelay": {
      "url": "@Model.McpUrl",
      "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer hr_svc_…" }
    }
  }
}</code></pre></div>
        <h3>Tools</h3>
        <table class="table">
            <tr><td class="mono">publish_notification</td><td>tags, title, body, url, priority, markdown, diagram, imageUrl, notificationId. Returns matched, live and queued device counts plus the detail page URL.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">list_tags</td><td>Hashtags that currently have subscribers, with counts and whether this credential may publish to each.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">list_notifications</td><td>Recent notifications, optionally for one hashtag. Needs <code>notifications:read</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">get_notification</td><td>One notification with its markdown, diagram and delivery counts. Needs <code>notifications:read</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">list_endpoints</td><td>Devices, online state and subscriptions. Needs <code>endpoints:read</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">set_subscriptions</td><td>Add, remove or replace a device's hashtags. Needs <code>subscriptions:write</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">list_approvers</td><td>Workspace members who may be named as approvers (account id, display name, role; no emails). Needs <code>approvals:create</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">request_approval</td><td>subject, targetUserIds, expiresAt, idempotencyKey, description, tags, requesterCorrelationId. Returns at once with status <code>pending</code> and the approval page URL; it does not wait for the decision. Needs <code>approvals:create</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">get_approval</td><td>The authoritative status, version, timestamps and decision of an approval this app requested. <code>proceed</code> is true only for <code>approved</code>. Needs <code>approvals:create</code>.</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p>There is no tool to approve or reject. A designated person decides on the signed-in approval page; an agent's credential is refused on the decision route.</p>
        <p>The resource <code>hashrelay://workspace</code> tells the agent its workspace, permissions, allowed hashtags and remaining quota. Read-tool results are marked as untrusted content written by other publishers, and a publish-only account cannot read them at all, which keeps a misled agent from being fed instructions through notifications.</p>
        <p>Browser-based clients need their origin listed in <code>HashRelay:McpAllowedOrigins</code>; requests with any other <code>Origin</code> header are refused. Hosted assistants that use OAuth (claude.ai and Claude Desktop connectors, ChatGPT) discover the authorization server through <code>/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource</code>: paste the MCP URL, sign in, and approve the consent screen. Each approval creates a service account named after the app, so the same policy, quota and activity log apply to it, and <strong>Disconnect</strong> on that service account revokes every token at once. Tokens last one hour and refresh automatically; clients register themselves (RFC 7591) and must use PKCE.</p>

        <h2 id="dotnet">.NET client</h2>
        <p>The <code>HashRelay.Client</code> library (in the repository) wraps the API and hub used by the CLI and agent:</p>
        <div class="code"><button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button><pre><code>using HashRelay.Client;
using HashRelay.Contracts;

using var api = new HashRelayApi(new Uri("@url/"), Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("HASHRELAY_KEY"));
var result = await api.PublishAsync(new PublishRequest(["builds"], "Build complete", "212 tests passed"));
Console.WriteLine($"matched {result.MatchedEndpoints}, live {result.DeliveredLive}");</code></pre></div>
    </article>
</div>
